      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "International organizations often struggle to protect specific national interests because they are inherently built on consensus among sovereign states, which prioritizes collective agreement over individual power. Veto powers, uneven enforcement mechanisms, and the reliance on state consent mean that powerful members can effectively shield themselves or allies from scrutiny. Furthermore, the UN's structure often demands broad international buy-in, making decisive action on narrow national disputes incredibly difficult to achieve.",
